/*!
    \page architecture.html
    \title Architecture
    \previouspage Configuration
    \nextpage Project Structure

    This chapter gives a new developer a fast mental model of the program: the major
    modules, how the UI reaches the backend, the threading and process boundaries, and
    how data and commands flow. Each named class links to its page in the
    \l {OpcUaManager API Reference}.

    \section1 Build targets

    The application is built from three static libraries, two executables, and two QML
    modules (see \l {Project Structure}):

    \list
        \li \b{OpcUaCore} — the OPC UA client core, item models, persistence, and the
            client project layer. Links Qt Core, Network, OpcUa, and Sql.
        \li \b{ServerProjectCore} — the server-project domain model (\c .uaserver),
            serializer, validator, and NodeSet2 I/O. Links \e{Qt Core only} so the
            headless runtime can reuse it without the client stack.
        \li \b{OpcUaQmlApi} — the QML-facing facades. Depends on OpcUaCore and
            ServerProjectCore.
        \li \b{appOpcUaManager} — the GUI executable (\c app/).
        \li \b{OpcUaServerRuntime} — the headless open62541 server executable (\c runtime/).
        \li QML modules \b{OpcUaManager} (screens) and \b{Base} (reusable controls).
    \endlist

    \section1 The QML-to-C++ boundary

    The C++ backend is exposed to QML through \e{context properties}, not through
    registered QML types. The application engine sets these on the root context:

    \list
        \li \c cppManagerOpcUa → \l OpcUaManager (client operations and models)
        \li \c cppServerStudio → \l ServerStudio (server design and runtime control)
        \li \c cppProjectManager → the client-project facade
        \li \c cppAppEngine → \l AppEngine (application services)
        \li \c cppAppInfo → \l AppInfo, \c cppLocale → \l LocaleController,
            \c cppLicenseModel → \l LicenseModel
    \endlist

    Because context properties need no import, the reusable \l {Base QML Types}{Base}
    components stay free of any C++ import; only the application layer binds them to the
    facades. There are deliberately no \c{Cpp.*} QML modules in this code base.

    \section1 OPC UA client: GUI thread versus worker thread

    The client is split across two threads to keep the UI responsive:

    \list
        \li \l OpcUaManager runs on the GUI thread. It owns the models
            (\l OpcUaModel, \l DataAccessModel, \l AttributesModel, \l TrendModel)
            and is the single object QML talks to.
        \li \l OpcUaService runs on a dedicated worker thread created by \l AppEngine.
            It owns the \c QOpcUaClient and performs discovery, connect/disconnect,
            browsing, reads, writes, and subscriptions.
    \endlist

    The two communicate through queued signals, so the network- and PKI-facing work
    never blocks rendering. Decoding of structured (CODESYS-style) values is handled by
    a helper that browses the instance subtree.

    \section1 OPC UA server: two processes

    The designed server runs as a \e separate process, not inside the GUI:

    \list
        \li \l ServerStudio (GUI) owns the in-memory server project and the editing
            models, and drives the runtime.
        \li \l ServerRuntimeController owns a \c QProcess running \b OpcUaServerRuntime.
            The runtime embeds open62541, builds the address space from the project,
            applies security, and simulates values.
        \li The two communicate over a simple contract: the runtime prints a \c READY
            line with its endpoint on stdout and accepts \c STOP on stdin. A separate
            \c QLocalSocket diagnostics side-channel streams live session/uptime JSON.
    \endlist

    \e{Open in Client} connects the built-in client to the running local server by
    handing its discovery URL to \l OpcUaManager.

    \section1 Data and command flow (client read)

    \list 1
        \li QML calls a method on \c cppManagerOpcUa (\l OpcUaManager).
        \li \l OpcUaManager forwards the request to \l OpcUaService via a queued signal.
        \li \l OpcUaService performs the OPC UA call on the worker thread.
        \li The result returns as a queued signal; \l OpcUaManager updates the relevant
            model (\l OpcUaModel / \l DataAccessModel), which QML is bound to.
    \endlist

    \section1 Configuration and storage

    \list
        \li \l AppPaths decides installed-versus-portable locations and seeds writable
            storage on first run.
        \li Settings live in an INI store; client projects (\c .uaproj) and server
            projects (\c .uaserver) are JSON, written atomically by their serializers.
        \li Monitored nodes are persisted in a SQLite database (\l NodeDatabase).
        \li \l LocaleController installs and live-switches the UI language.
    \endlist
*/
